A timestamp records when something happened. JavaScript's `Date.now()` gives a timestamp measured in milliseconds since a fixed reference date. A millisecond is one thousandth of a second. To measure a duration, subtract the earlier timestamp from the later one. The result is elapsed time, not a clock reading. Both numbers must use the same unit and reference.

```javascript
const recorded = Date.now();
console.log(typeof recorded);
console.log(Number.isFinite(recorded));
```
This prints number and true. Date.now takes no arguments and returns milliseconds since 1970-01-01T00:00:00Z. Its exact number changes with the wall clock, so the example checks the result kind rather than promising a fixed timestamp.

Here is a complete timer calculation using supplied practice times.
```javascript
let startedAt = null;
let elapsed = null;
function signal(now) {
  startedAt = now;
  elapsed = null;
}
function clickAt(now) {
  if (startedAt !== null) {
    elapsed = Math.max(0, now - startedAt);
  }
}
signal(2000);
clickAt(2180);
console.log(elapsed);
```
It prints 180. The first call records 2000; the second subtracts it from 2180. The functions change the outer let variables, so they do not need return statements to save the result.

A reset prepares another round.
```javascript
let startedAt = null;
let elapsed = null;
function signal(now) {
  startedAt = now;
  elapsed = null;
}
function clickAt(now) {
  if (startedAt !== null) {
    elapsed = Math.max(0, now - startedAt);
  }
}
function reset() {
  startedAt = null;
  elapsed = null;
}
clickAt(1500);
console.log(elapsed);
signal(2000);
clickAt(2200);
reset();
console.log(startedAt, elapsed);
```
This displays null, then `null null`. The early click stored nothing; reset cleared both values after the later round.

